             "data": {
              "content": "Etymology-content"
             },
             "content": "Inherited from Byzantine Greek γάτα (gáta), an 11th-century parallel form of κάττα (kátta), from Medieval Latin gatta, from Late Latin catta.\nOr, from Hellenistic κάττα (kátta) with sound change from liaison with the accusative of the feminine article τήν (tḗn) /tin ˈkata > tiŋˈɡata > ti ˈɣata/.\nThe mediaeval or Hellenistic form κάττα (kátta) and the simplified spelling κάτα (káta) survives in modern form κάτα (káta), diminutives (as κατσούλα (katsoúla)), dialectal and regional. From Late Latin catta. Also see the masculine γάτος (gátos), from Late Latin cattus."
            }
